---
import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import { refById } from '../../data/references';
import acu from '../../data/sim/acupoints.json';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const repo = 'https://github.com/ejhong/knots/blob/main';
const pct = (x: number) => `${Math.round(100 * x)}%`;
const cite = (id: string) => {
  const ref = refById(id);
  if (!ref) return id;
  const parts = ref.authors.split(',')[0].split(' et al')[0].trim().split(' ');
  const last = parts[parts.length - 1];
  const who = /^\p{Lu}{1,3}$/u.test(last) ? parts.slice(0, -1).join(' ') : last; // "Burton AC" or "Michael Edward Johnson"
  return `<a class="cite" href="${base}/library/#${id}">${who} ${ref.year}</a>`;
};

// Are the perforators where the points are? (sim/exploratory/2026-11-02-acupoints-perforators.py). Until 14 Nov 2026 this
// was a section of the Research page; the position paper cites it as research/#points, which lands on its card there.
const ACU_LINES: Record<string, { where: string; with: string; ends: boolean }> = {
  anterior: { where: 'Front of the lower leg', with: 'anterior tibial perforators · ST36–40, GB36–39', ends: true },
  lateral: { where: 'Behind the fibula', with: 'peroneal perforators · GB35, BL58, BL59', ends: true },
  front: { where: 'Front of the thigh', with: 'the thigh perforator on the hip-to-kneecap line · ST32–34', ends: true },
  forearm: { where: 'Forearm', with: 'radial artery perforators · LU6–8', ends: false },
  back: { where: 'Back of the calf', with: 'medial sural perforators · BL55–57', ends: false },
};
const acuLines = acu.lines.map((l) => ({ ...l, ...ACU_LINES[l.name.split(' ')[0]] }));
const x2 = (v: number) => `${v.toFixed(2)}×`;
const p2 = (v: number) => v.toFixed(2);
const baseRate = (r: string) => `${pct(acu.base_rate[r as '1'].lo)}–${pct(acu.base_rate[r as '1'].hi)}`;
const ptScore = (name: string, code: string) => (acu.lines.find((l) => l.name.startsWith(name))!.points as unknown as Record<string, number>)[code];

const index = [
  { id: 'numbers', label: 'in three numbers' },
  { id: 'same', label: 'the same in everyone?' },
  { id: 'claims', label: 'what has been claimed' },
  { id: 'test', label: 'a test against chance' },
  { id: 'decide', label: 'a study that might decide it' },
];
---
<Doc
  title="Are the perforators where the points are?"
  kicker="Research · perforators and acupoints"
  current="research"
  lede="Two questions. Do the major perforators come through the fascia in the same places in everyone? And do the acupoints of Chinese medicine sit on them? The zones are shared; the vessels are not. The points sit a little nearer the perforators than chance would put them, too little to tell from chance with the evidence there is."
  description="Do the major perforators come through the fascia in the same places in everyone, and do the acupoints of Chinese medicine sit on them? The evidence, a test against chance, and a study that might decide it."
  glyph="穴"
  index={index}
  wide
>
  <section id="numbers" class="sec full" data-glyph="数">
    <h2>In three numbers</h2>
    <div class="facts pt-facts">
      <p class="fact"><span class="big">2–4 cm</span><span class="fl">how far one perforator cluster spreads between people: an SD of 6–11% of the shin's length on CT angiography <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('martin2013')})`} />, about one to three cun.</span></p>
      <p class="fact"><span class="big">1–3.6 cm</span><span class="fl">how far doctors' marks for one point spread: 95% of 71 doctors' marks fell within 2.7 cm² (PC6) to 41.4 cm² (ST38) <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('molsberger2012')})`} />, a circle of that radius.</span></p>
      <p class="fact"><span class="big">{x2(acu.ratio.median)}</span><span class="fl">the perforator density at {acu.points} WHO points, against points placed at random on the same five lines (median of {acu.variants} variants; p {p2(acu.p.min)}–{p2(acu.p.max)}, below 0.05 in {pct(acu.p.share_below_05)} of them). A map whose points sat on the clusters would score {x2(acu.perfect.min)}–{x2(acu.perfect.max)}, p &lt; 0.0001: the test could see it.</span></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="same" class="sec full" data-glyph="同">
    <h2>The same in everyone?</h2>
    <div class="two">
      <div>
        <p class="find"><strong>The zones, yes.</strong> About forty source arteries, and on average 374 major perforators in each
          body, follow the connective-tissue framework <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('taylor1987')})`} />: the arteries are constant and
          the single vessels variable <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('morris2010')})`} />. Some places almost always have one. Perforators
          gather at the middle of the line from the hip bone to the kneecap (74.6% in its middle three tenths; none at all in 6.6% of
          thighs) <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('ulatowski2012')})`} />. An internal mammary perforator was present on both sides in
          every case, about 1.8 cm from the edge of the breastbone <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('kanoi2017')})`} />, the largest in the
          first or second rib space in three cases of four <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('okumura2016')})`} />. The lowest
          posterior tibial perforator lay 3.5–8.2 cm above the inner ankle in all thirty legs <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('bulla2015')})`} />.</p>
        <p class="find"><strong>The vessels, no.</strong> Each cluster spreads over centimetres <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('martin2013')})`} />,
          and the two sides of one body differ. On the thighs the average place agrees within 3% of thigh length but the number of
          perforators does not <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('lu2019')})`} />; the largest internal mammary perforator is in the same rib
          space on both sides in only 49% <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('okumura2016')})`} />; the abdomen's perforators do not correspond
          between sides <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('rozen2010b')})`} />, nor does the branching of their source artery{' '}
          <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('rozen2010')})`} />. Surgeons map each side before they cut.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="find"><strong>What it means for knots.</strong> No map of knots can hold across people, only a map of the zones
          where perforators crowd: the atlas's perforators are placed that way, by density, and are representative. Within one
          person the perforators do not move. So if knots sit at perforators, or at the nerves that pass beside them, one person's
          knots should come and go among a fixed set of places, their own map, and a knot that seems to move should be one place
          letting go and another taking up. A theory that puts a knot wherever stress is held needs no such stations. That needs
          one person, a Doppler map and a few months of marked knots.</p>
        <p class="find"><strong>Points are proportional too.</strong> Acupoints are placed by proportional inches (cun) between
          landmarks, and surgeons place perforators the same way, as fractions of a segment. That is the frame to compare them in,
          but the spread that remains after scaling is still centimetres on both sides: a fixed map of points could only ever
          match the zones.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="claims" class="sec full" data-glyph="言">
    <h2>What has been claimed</h2>
    <div class="two">
      <div>
        <p class="find">Heine reported that about 80% of acupoints lie where a vessel–nerve bundle perforates the fascia{' '}
          <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('heine1988')})`} />; the figure is widely repeated, and its method cannot be checked from the
          record. Earlier, dissection beneath 201 points found a nerve at 29%, a vessel at 34% and both at 37%: no one structure common
          to all <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('bossy1975')})`} />. A multicentre dissection of 100 lower-leg points found vessel–nerve
          bundles at two <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('maurer2019')})`} />. The two may not have looked at the same layer of fascia,
          which is worth settling against Heine's original. Most points do lie on the connective-tissue planes between muscles{' '}
          <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('langevin2002')})`} />, which are where many perforators run, but planes are not perforators.</p>
        <p class="find">One study used controls: thermography found a perforator at 87.5% of 144 thigh points and at 18.1% of
          control points 4 cm along the same channel, in six people, unblinded <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('alvarezprats2019')})`} />.
          A dissection of two arms found a perforating vessel at all 30 points studied <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('meng2024')})`} />;
          a Doppler study found “a certain correspondence”, with physicians' marks for one point up to 16.6 mm apart{' '}
          <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('guo2024')})`} />. None compared the points with points placed at random.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="find"><strong>Why chance matters.</strong> If the 374 major perforators were scattered evenly over an adult's skin
          (1.6–2 m²), a spot chosen at random would lie within 1 cm of one {baseRate('1')} of the time, within 2 cm {baseRate('2')},
          within 3 cm {baseRate('3')}. Points are found only to within one to three centimetres{' '}
          <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('molsberger2012')}; ${cite('aird2002')})`} />, and smaller perforators are far denser, so “a
          perforator near the point” is common by chance. The comparison of trigger points with acupoints fell into the same trap:
          a 71% correspondence <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('melzack1977')})`} />, later put nearer 18–19%{' '}
          <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('birch2003')})`} />. A coincidence rate means nothing without the rate for points that are not
          points, and without saying which vessels count.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="test" class="sec full" data-glyph="検">
    <h2>A test against chance</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      On five lines of the limbs, a measured perforator cluster and a run of WHO points lie along the same septum. Both are written
      as a fraction of the segment, the perforators with their spread between people, the points by their cun. The perforator
      density at the points is compared with the density at points placed at random on the same line, in {acu.variants} variants:
      the papers that give a percentage without saying from which end were run both ways, and the location error and the cun
      conversion were varied.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap pt-table">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Perforators · points</th><th>Density at the points, against random</th><th>p</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {acuLines.map((l) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{l.where}</td>
              <td>{l.with}</td>
              <td>{l.ratio.min.toFixed(1)}–{x2(l.ratio.max)}{l.ends ? ' *' : ''}</td>
              <td class="nw">{p2(l.p.min)}–{p2(l.p.max)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="caption">* The paper gives a percentage without saying which end it is measured from; the range spans both readings.</p>
    <div class="two">
      <div>
        <p class="find"><strong>Some points sit on a cluster.</strong> SP6, three cun above the inner ankle, is about{' '}
          {acu.medial.points.SP6.toFixed(1)} cm up on a {Math.round(acu.medial.leg_cm)} cm shin; the lowest posterior tibial perforator
          lies at a median 6.75 cm <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('bulla2015')})`} />. LU6 and LU7 sit at 58% and 12.5% of the forearm; the
          radial artery's two clusters at 61.7% and 17.6% <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('saintcyr2010')})`} /> (density {x2(ptScore('forearm', 'LU6'))}{' '}
          and {x2(ptScore('forearm', 'LU7'))} the line's average). BL56 is about 12.5 cm below the knee crease; the medial sural
          perforators 11.4 ± 2.7 cm <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('kao2010')})`} /> ({x2(ptScore('back', 'BL56'))}). ST32 lies on the
          very line surgeons draw to find the thigh's perforator.</p>
        <p class="find"><strong>Others do not.</strong> ST33, ST34, BL55 and BL57 lie between clusters. The abdomen's dominant
          perforator lies at a median 3.3 cm out and 0.9 cm below the navel <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('saad2014')})`} />,
          between the Kidney line (half a cun out) and the Stomach line (two cun). The dominant lumbar perforator reaches the fat
          69.5 mm from the midline, mostly at the third lumbar level <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('hamdi2016')})`} />: on the course
          of the outer Bladder line, where the WHO places no point.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="find"><strong>Across the limb, they agree; that is partly built in.</strong> Many channels run along the septa that
          perforators leave by: the points “behind the edge of the shinbone” are on the posterior tibial perforators' septum, the
          Lung points at the wrist over the radial artery. But points and septa are both defined by the same muscles and bones, so
          that agreement is not independent evidence. Along the line, where it would be, the points do slightly better than
          random and much worse than a map placed on the clusters.</p>
        <p class="find"><strong>Its limits.</strong> The perforator positions are averages from different bodies, five to sixty
          limbs a study; there are {acu.points} points on five lines; the shin's cun and the anatomists' tibial height are not quite
          the same length. It is a map from averages, not a measurement. Not shown, not excluded.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="decide" class="sec full" data-glyph="決">
    <h2>A study that might decide it</h2>
    <p class="sub">
      The same study as <a href={`${base}/research/#experiment-10`}>experiment 10</a> on the Research page, in more detail.
    </p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><strong>In the same people.</strong> Perforators mapped by thermal camera as the skin rewarms after a mild cold
        challenge; the first hot spots lie over perforators that Doppler hears and CT angiography shows{' '}
        <Fragment set:html={`(${cite('weum2016b')})`} />. A share of the hot spots, and every point without one, checked by colour
        Doppler. Points marked by several practitioners by the WHO's proportional rules, out of the thermal reader's sight and
        never inked before imaging.</li>
      <li><strong>Against chance, at every distance.</strong> Positions in proportional units; the share of points with a
        perforator within 0.5, 1, 2 and 3 cm, against random points in the same region, the whole map shifted along the limb, and
        points at the same level off the channel. The vessels that count (those through the deep fascia, of a stated size) and the
        distances are fixed before looking. Twenty people give thousands of pairs; bias, not numbers, is the risk.</li>
      <li><strong>Data that already exist.</strong> CT angiograms taken before abdominal flap surgery record perforators against
        the navel by the thousand. Laying the Kidney, Stomach and Spleen lines over them is a study someone could do from records.</li>
      <li><strong>What it would not settle.</strong> A cutaneous nerve comes through the same opening as the vessel, so a match
        cannot tell perforators from nerves. A recording at a release might (<a href={`${base}/research/#experiment-2`}>experiment
        2</a>): if a tender point is a perforator held shut, the same camera should see it as a late or missing hot spot that warms
        within seconds of a felt release.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="caption">
      The atlas draws the fourteen channels and the 361 points by the WHO's proportional rules (<a href={`${base}/atlas/?map=meridians`}>channels and points</a>);
      the traditions behind them are on <a href={`${base}/traditions/#china`}>Traditions</a>. The test's code is in the repository
      (<a href={`${repo}/sim/exploratory/2026-11-02-acupoints-perforators.py`}>acupoints-perforators.py</a>), every number with its source.
      Any measurement with people belongs under the site's moderation guidance (see <a href={`${base}/about/#moderation`}>About</a>).
    </p>
  </section>
</Doc>

<style>
